.\" Manpage for auto-updates.
.\" Process with: groff -man -Tascii auto-updates.8
.TH AUTO-UPDATES 8 "29 September 2026" "auto-updates 1.2.2" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
auto-updates \- Automated system updates manager and systemd integration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auto-updates
[\fICOMMAND\fR | \fIOPTION\fR]
.br
.B auto-updates
\fBmode\fR \fIMODE\fR
.br
.B auto-updates
\fBset-reboot\fR \fIPOLICY\fR
.br
.B auto-updates
\fBrun\fR [\fB\-\-security\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B auto-updates
is a CLI utility and systemd service manager that provides turnkey automated package
maintenance for Linux distributions, including Fedora, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, CentOS
Stream, RHEL, Debian, and Ubuntu.
.PP
By default upon installation,
.B auto-updates
configures and enables daily security updates at 03:30 AM in the local system timezone.
The update mode, execution schedule, reboot policy, and execution behavior can be
inspected and altered using the command-line interface or by editing
.BR /etc/auto-updates/auto-updates.conf .
.PP
It is fully cross-platform, automatically detecting the host backend: \fBAPT\fR
(\fBunattended-upgrades\fR on Debian/Ubuntu), \fBDNF5\fR (Fedora 41+, RHEL 10, Rocky 10),
or \fBDNF4\fR (RHEL/Rocky/Alma 8/9), and cleanly disabling any conflicting stock systemd
timers.
.SH UPDATE MODES
.TP
.B security
Downloads and applies only packages marked as security advisories every day at the
configured schedule time (default: 03:30 AM). Recommended for high-uptime servers.
.TP
.B all
Performs a full system upgrade of all packages (bugfixes, features, enhancements, and
security) every day at the scheduled time. Matches the default behavior of standard
\fBdnf-automatic\fR in default mode.
.TP
.B weekly-all \fR(alias \fBall-weekly\fR)
A hybrid schedule that runs fast daily security updates from Monday through Saturday,
and executes a complete system upgrade of all packages once per week on the configured
day (default: Sunday at 03:30 AM). Recommended for workstations and development hosts.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status \fR(alias \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-status\fR)
Displays an interactive status dashboard showing the systemd timer state, configured
mode, execution schedule, backend implementation, remote metadata refresh status,
configured reboot policy, active build protection status, and live system reboot requirements.
.TP
.BR set-mode " " \fIMODE\fR " | " \fBmode\fR " " \fIMODE\fR
Sets the update mode to \fBsecurity\fR, \fBall\fR, or \fBweekly-all\fR. Requires root privileges.
.TP
.B enable \fR(alias \fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-enable\fR)
Enables and immediately starts the \fBauto-updates.timer\fR systemd unit. Requires root privileges.
.TP
.B disable \fR(alias \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-disable\fR)
Stops and disables the \fBauto-updates.timer\fR systemd unit. Requires root privileges.
.TP
.BR run " [" \-\-security " | " \-\-all ] " (alias " \fB\-r\fR )
Executes an immediate manual update transaction. If invoked without flags, it executes
according to the current day's policy. Use \fB\-\-security\fR or \fB\-\-all\fR to override.
Requires root privileges.
.TP
.B check \fR(alias \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-check\fR)
Performs a dry-run check for available updates without downloading or applying them.
.TP
.BR set-time " " \fIHH:MM\fR
Updates the daily execution schedule time in 24-hour local time format (e.g., \fB04:00\fR)
and reapplies the systemd timer configuration. Requires root privileges.
.TP
.BR set-day " " \fIDAY\fR
Sets the day of the week on which full system upgrades are performed when running in
\fBweekly-all\fR mode (e.g., \fBSun\fR, \fBSat\fR). Requires root privileges.
.TP
.BR set-reboot " " \fI{never|when-needed|when-changed}\fR " (alias " \fB\-\-reboot\fR )
Configures the system reboot policy after automated updates. Valid policies are:
.RS
.TP
.B never
Never automatically reboot the system (default). Recommended for developer machines,
workstations, and production hosts requiring manual maintenance windows.
.TP
.B when-needed
Automatically reboots the system only if updated packages require a restart to take effect
(e.g., updates to the Linux kernel, systemd, glibc, or core system libraries).
.TP
.B when-changed
Automatically reboots the system whenever any package update is installed (recommended
for appliance or kiosk hosts).
.RE
.TP
.BR set-reboot-delay " " \fIMINUTES\fR " (alias " \fBset-delay\fR )
Configures the delay before rebooting when a reboot policy (\fBwhen-needed\fR or
\fBwhen-changed\fR) triggers. Setting this to \fB0\fR initiates an immediate reboot via
\fBsystemctl reboot\fR (recommended for virtual machines, containers, and servers). Setting
this to \fBN\fR schedules a reboot in \fBN\fR minutes using \fBshutdown -r +N\fR with
broadcast wall notifications. Default is \fB0\fR.
.TP
.BR logs " [" \fIN\fR ] " (alias " \fB\-l\fR )
Displays the last \fIN\fR lines of \fB/var/log/auto-updates.log\fR (default: 40 lines).
.TP
.B help \fR(alias \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR)
Displays a quick command-line usage summary and examples.
.TP
.B version \fR(alias \fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR)
Displays the installed program version.
.SH BUILD PROCESS AND INHIBITOR PROTECTION
When an automated reboot policy (\fBwhen-needed\fR or \fBwhen-changed\fR) is active and
\fBDEFER_REBOOT_IF_BUSY=true\fR is configured,
.B auto-updates
inspects the host for in-flight compilation and system tasks before issuing a reboot command.
.PP
The safety scanner verifies:
.IP \(bu 2
Active packaging and compilation tools: \fBrpmbuild\fR, \fBmock\fR, \fBmake\fR, \fBninja\fR,
\fBcargo\fR, \fBgcc\fR, \fBg++\fR, \fBclang\fR, \fBrustc\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Active containerized builds: \fBpodman build\fR, \fBdocker build\fR, \fBbuildah\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
System inhibitors: locks registered with \fBsystemd-inhibit\fR for shutdown or sleep operations.
.PP
If any active build processes or inhibitors are detected, the reboot is safely deferred until
the next scheduled update cycle, and an advisory notice is logged to \fB/var/log/auto-updates.log\fR.
.SH VIRTUAL MACHINE AND HYPERVISOR CONSIDERATIONS
When running \fBauto-updates\fR inside a virtual machine (e.g., GNOME Boxes, QEMU/KVM,
virt-manager, or VirtualBox), automated reboots require specific hypervisor settings to
ensure the VM powers back on:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGNOME Boxes Background Execution\fR: In GNOME Boxes, VMs running in the background can be
suspended or stopped when their display window is closed. Ensure \fB"Run in background"\fR is
enabled in the virtual machine's Preferences / Resources tab.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBImmediate Reboot (Delay 0)\fR: Keep \fBREBOOT_DELAY=0\fR (default). A delayed reboot
(\fBshutdown -r +5\fR) can leave an unattended guest in a session-timeout limbo state under
desktop hypervisors. An immediate reboot via \fBsystemctl reboot\fR cleanly resets the virtual
ACPI hardware.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHost Power Management\fR: Desktop hosts configured to enter low-power sleep or suspend
overnight will freeze guest VM execution. Ensure host sleep is inhibited or scheduled outside
the update maintenance window (\fBSCHEDULE_TIME\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLibvirt Lifecycle Policy\fR: Verify that the guest domain XML specifies
\fB<on_reboot>restart</on_reboot>\fR (not \fBdestroy\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/auto-updates/auto-updates.conf
Primary configuration file containing update modes, schedules, and reboot policies.
.TP
.B /etc/logrotate.d/auto-updates
Log rotation rules ensuring log files are archived and rotated weekly.
.TP
.B /usr/bin/auto-updates
The user-facing CLI utility.
.TP
.B /usr/libexec/auto-updates-runner
The systemd execution wrapper that applies configurations, verifies inhibitors, and runs DNF.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/systemd/system/auto-updates.service
The systemd one-shot service unit executed by the timer.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/systemd/system/auto-updates.timer
The systemd calendar timer triggering update runs. Configured with \fBPersistent=true\fR.
.TP
.B /var/log/auto-updates.log
Detailed execution log file.
